course: biology
lesson: 50
title: How animals talk
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Animals do not speak words like us, but they send each other messages all the time with sounds, dances, smells, light and the way they hold their bodies.
explain: A honeybee that has found flowers dances a figure of eight on the comb, and the angle and length of her waggle tell her sisters which way to fly and how far, a map made of movement.
explain: Your cat meowing at the fridge door is talking to you, not to other cats: adult cats rarely meow to each other, so the meow is a language cats invented for people.
explain: Cartoon animals chat in full sentences and every dog with a wagging tail is happy; real messages are simpler but cleverer, and a stiff, high, fast wag can mean a dog is tense, so ask the owner before you touch.
fact: A honeybee forager dances a figure of eight on the comb; the direction of the waggle run shows the angle of the flowers from the Sun and its length shows the distance, worked out by Karl von Frisch, who shared a Nobel Prize in 1973.
fact: Vervet monkeys have different alarm calls for leopards, eagles and snakes: at the leopard call they run up trees, at the eagle call they dive into bushes, and at the snake call they stand and look at the ground, because each predator attacks in a different way; young vervets sometimes call at harmless birds and learn the right use from adults over time.
fact: Prairie dogs give alarm calls that seem to change for different predators, and some studies suggest they carry details such as size or colour, though scientists still argue how much.
fact: A loose, wide tail wag is usually friendly, but a stiff, high, fast wag can mean a dog is tense; dogs talk with their whole body, ears, tail, hackles, mouth and how they stand.
fact: Adult cats rarely meow to each other: meowing is mostly how cats talk to people, and cats purr when content but sometimes also when hurt or unwell.
fact: Male humpback whales sing long songs of repeated themes for hours; males in one region sing nearly the same song, and new songs can spread across the Pacific over a few years.
fact: Elephants make deep rumbles, many too low for us to hear, called infrasound, which carry several kilometres and may be felt through their feet and trunk; an elephant may stand still with its ears spread out to listen for them.
fact: In spring male songbirds such as blackbirds sing at dawn to claim territory and be noticed, and young birds learn their songs by listening and practising, much like a baby learning to talk.
fact: Ants that find food lay a scent trail of chemicals called pheromones on the way home, and other ants smell it with their antennae and follow it.
fact: Fireflies are beetles that make cold light in their tails; each kind has its own flash code, and some females copy another kind's code to lure males and eat them.
fact: An African grey parrot named Alex, studied by Irene Pepperberg for 30 years, could name dozens of objects, colours and shapes and count up to six, though most talking parrots just copy sounds.
fact: Each bottlenose dolphin has its own signature whistle, a bit like a name, which others can copy to call it, and dolphins seem to remember old companions' whistles for 20 years or more.
exercise: What does the waggle dance of a honeybee tell the other bees? | Which way the flowers are and how far away | How old the dancing bee is and how many days it has worked | That the queen is hungry | That it is going to rain
exercise: What does the length of the waggle run show? | How far away the flowers are | How many flowers there are in the patch | What colour the flowers in the patch are | How sweet the nectar in the flowers is
exercise: Why do vervet monkeys need different alarm calls for a leopard and an eagle? | Each predator attacks differently, so a different escape is needed | Leopards and eagles speak different languages, so each needs its own call | One call is for adults and one for babies | They do not, one call means every danger
exercise: What do vervets do when they hear the eagle alarm? | Look up and dive into bushes | Run straight up the nearest tree | Stand up and look at the ground | Jump into the river to swim off
exercise: What do scientists think prairie dog alarm calls may carry? | Details such as the predator's kind, size or colour | The names of every single prairie dog in the whole colony | Directions to the nearest food | Nothing, they are random squeaks
exercise: What can a stiff, high, fast tail wag mean in a dog? | The dog may be tense or ready to defend itself | The dog is happy and ready to play | The dog is asleep | The dog is hungry for treats
exercise: Who do cats mostly meow to? | People | Other cats | Birds they are hunting | Nobody, only in their sleep
exercise: Does purring always mean a cat is happy? | No, cats sometimes purr when hurt or unwell | Yes, always, a purr only ever means a happy cat | Yes, it means it wants food | No, purring always means pain
exercise: Which whales sing long songs for hours? | Male humpback whales | Baby blue whales at night | Female sharks out at sea | All the fish in the sea
exercise: What is infrasound in elephants? | Deep rumbles too low for us to hear, carrying several kilometres | Squeaks far too high for us to hear, used only between mothers and babies | Messages sent by stamping with the trunk | The sound of an elephant drinking
exercise: Why does a blackbird sing loudly at dawn in spring? | To claim its territory and be noticed | To wake up all the people in the house nearby | To warn of rain later in the day | Because it is too cold to sleep
exercise: How do young songbirds learn their songs? | By listening to adults and practising | They know them all from the egg | By copying the radio | They never learn, they only squeak
exercise: How does a line of ants find a crumb on the kitchen floor? | They follow a scent trail of pheromones laid by other ants | They see it from far away with their sharp eyes and big lenses | Their queen tells them by sound | They each find it by luck
exercise: How do fireflies find their own kind in the dark? | Each kind has its own flash code | They all flash in exactly the same way | They follow the light of the moon | They listen for each other's buzz
exercise: What could Alex the African grey parrot do? | Name dozens of objects, colours and shapes and count up to six | Read whole books aloud | Speak three human languages fluently | Only copy one word over and over
exercise: What is a dolphin's signature whistle? | Its own whistle, a bit like a name | The sound it makes when it is hungry | A warning about sharks only | The song that all dolphins sing together
exercise: How long do dolphins seem to remember old friends' whistles? | For 20 years or more | For 3 seconds | For one day | For one week or so
test: «dog's wagging its tail so it's friendly, I'm gonna pet it» What is the best advice? | Ask the owner first and look at the whole body; a stiff wag can mean tense | Fine, any wag means happy | Fine, pet it on the head straight away, mate | Only pet dogs that are not wagging
test: In a cartoon, two cats sit on a fence and meow a long chat to each other. What is more true of real adult cats? | They rarely meow to each other; meowing is mostly for people | They meow to each other all day long | They only talk by purring | They speak with their whiskers in words
test: «my parrot says hello so he knows what it means, obvs» What is the careful answer? | Most parrots just copy sounds; some, like Alex, understood some words | Every parrot understands all it says, they're as clever as people, obviously | No parrot ever understands anything | Parrots understand only if they speak quietly
test: What would happen if you wiped your finger across a busy ant trail? | The ants would get confused there until they found the scent again | The ants would follow your finger home | Nothing, ants follow by sight | The ants would all turn round, go back to the nest and never come out again
test: «why's that elephant just standing there with its ears out, it's deaf or what» What may be going on? | It may be listening for deep rumbles from elephants far away | It is asleep standing up | It is cooling its ears only | It is deaf and just waiting there for the others to come back and help it
test: A young vervet gives the eagle call when a harmless pigeon flies over. What happens next, in real life? | It learns the right use from adults over time | It is thrown out of the group for good | It never makes the call again in its life | All the vervets climb trees
test: «whale songs are just random noises» What did scientists find? | Males in one region sing nearly the same song, and new songs spread | Each whale sings a random noise every time | Only one whale in the whole world sings, and the rest just listen to it | Whales copy the songs of ships
test: In a game, all fireflies flash the same pattern. What is wrong, and what could a sneaky female do? | Each kind has its own code; some females copy another kind's to eat males | Nothing, they all flash the same | Fireflies make light with tiny fires | Females never flash at all, so they sit in the dark and wait for a male to land
test: «the bee's just dancing for fun, bees love a boogie» What is right? | She is showing the way and distance to flowers | Right, bees dance to have fun | She is fanning to cool the hive | She is fighting another bee
test: «dolphins don't have names, they're animals» What did studies show? | Each bottlenose dolphin has a signature whistle, a bit like a name | Right, dolphins cannot tell each other apart at all, they all sound alike | Dolphins use human names they hear on boats | Only captive dolphins have names
test: A cat purrs loudly at the vet with a hurt paw. What could the purr mean? | Cats sometimes purr when hurt or unwell, not only when happy | It means the paw does not hurt at all and the cat is perfectly happy | It means the cat is angry at the vet | Purring heals any wound instantly
test: Why would one alarm call meaning just «danger» be worse for vervets than three different calls? | They would not know whether to climb, hide in bushes or watch the ground | One call would be louder and scare the predator | Three calls confuse the monkeys more | It would be better, because a simple call is quicker to give and the monkeys could all just run
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the waggle dance of the honeybee? | By the direction and length of the waggle run, it conveys the bearing and distance of food | It serves solely as a form of play among workers | It announces the death of the queen | It is performed exclusively in order to cool the hive in the heat of the summer months
exam: Identify the principal reason for which vervet monkeys employ distinct alarm calls for different predators. | Each predator attacks in a different manner, requiring a distinct escape | The predators are thereby informed that they have been observed and should withdraw | The calls serve to identify the individual making them | Each call is directed at a different group of monkeys
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the meowing of the domestic cat. | Adult cats seldom meow to one another, meowing chiefly to humans | Adult cats communicate with one another chiefly by means of the meow | The meow is produced only during sleep | The meow is employed exclusively to warn of predators and other dangers
exam: Ascertain the means whereby ants direct their fellows to a source of food. | By depositing a trail of chemical scent, termed pheromones | By means of loud calls audible at great distance | By a dance performed upon the comb | By flashes of light emitted from the abdomen in a fixed sequence
